PR #730  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Trap side effects must execute in fault-tolerant order (register saves before SP write) so a synchronous exception during context save/restore is recoverable; otherwise, partially-applied side effects corrupt state on re-execution.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/exception.S:41-114  save_registers
      sp is decremented (addi sp, sp, -(TF_SIZE)) on line 42 BEFORE any GPR save or CSR capture. A synchronous page fault during any subsequent sd/csrr instruction causes hardware to overwrite sepc/scause/stval with the fault context, while sp remains modified and the trapframe is only partially written. sscratch is cleared (line 100, csrw sscratch, zero) BEFORE sepc is read (line 101), so a nested fault in that window sees sscratch=0 and consumes the remaining trap state. No Smrnmi (mnepc/mncause) exists to preserve the original trap context across a nested fault, and no double-trap detection guards this window.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/exception.S:208-217  cpu_exception_handler
      The stvec entry point uses csrrw sp, sscratch, sp (line 209) to atomically swap sp with sscratch for privilege-mode detection. This modifies sp before any context save occurs. If a nested trap arrives at this exact instruction, sscratch already holds the prior sp value — the new trap's csrrw atomically swaps again, consuming sscratch and losing the original sp. The subsequent beqz branch (line 210) on the already-consumed sscratch value is a single point of failure with no recovery path. No Smrnmi hardware preserves machine-level trap state across this window.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/exception.S:219-225  cpu_exception_handler_supervisor
      Invokes save_registers(1) → do_trap_supervisor → load_registers(1) → sret with zero nested-trap guards. If a page fault occurs during save_registers (e.g., the kernel stack page for the trapframe is not resident), hardware overwrites the original sepc/scause/stval. The handler delivers to do_trap_supervisor which unconditionally panics on access faults (trap.c:334-339) and misaligned accesses (trap.c:341-346) — the exact fault types possible during context save/restore. Recovery is impossible because the original sepc is gone. sstatus SUM manipulation inside save_registers (line 107-108) modifies architectural state before the full context save completes.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/exception.S:227-235  cpu_exception_handler_user
      Invokes save_registers(0) → do_trap_user → do_ast → load_registers(0) → sret. Same non-restartable window as the supervisor handler. If a TLB miss occurs during user register save to the kernel stack (any sd instruction in the save_registers macro), the hardware overwrites sepc/scause/stval. The original user sepc (the faulting user instruction address) is lost, making clean signal delivery (SIGSEGV/SIGBUS) impossible because the kernel no longer knows which user instruction faulted. Both the save and restore paths lack double-trap protection.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/exception.S:116-182  load_registers
